<person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sebanz</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Bekkering</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Knoblich</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Joint action: bodies and minds moving together</article-title><source>Trends in Cognitive Sciences</source><year>2006</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>70</fpage><lpage>76</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.tics.2005.12.009</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Sebanz, N., Bekkering, H., &#x00026; Knoblich, G. (2006). Joint action: bodies and minds moving together. Trends in Cognitive Sciences,10, 70&#x02013;76. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16406326</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shibata</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Suzuki</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Gyoba</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cortical activity during the recognition of cooperative actions</article-title><source>NeuroReport</source><year>2007</year><volume>18</volume><fpage>697</fpage><lpage>701</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1097/WNR.0b013e3280d94375</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Shibata, H., Suzuki, M., &#x00026; Gyoba, J. (2007). Cortical activity during the recognition of cooperative actions. NeuroReport,18, 697&#x02013;701. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17426602</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schie</surname><given-names>HT</given-names></name><name><surname>Waterschoot</surname><given-names>BM</given-names></name><name><surname>Bekkering</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Understanding action beyond imitation: Reversed compatibility effects of action observation in imitation and joint action</article-title><source>Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance</source><year>2008</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>1493</fpage><lpage>1500</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1037/a0011750</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">van Schie, H. T., van Waterschoot, B. M., &#x00026; Bekkering, H. (2008). Understanding action beyond imitation: Reversed compatibility effects of action observation in imitation and joint action. Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance,34, 1493&#x02013;1500. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19045988</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR29"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waszak</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Hommel</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Allport</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Task-switching and long-term priming: Role of episodic stimulus-task bindings in taskshift costs</article-title><source>Cognitive Psychology</source><year>2003</year><volume>46</volume><fpage>361</fpage><lpage>413</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0010-0285(02)00520-0</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Waszak, F., Hommel, B., &#x00026; Allport, A. (2003). Task-switching and long-term priming: Role of episodic stimulus-task bindings in taskshift costs. Cognitive Psychology,46, 361&#x02013;413. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12809680</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waszak</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Hommel</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Allport</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Semantic generalization of stimulus-task bindings</article-title><source>Psychonomic Bulletin &#x00026; Review</source><year>2004</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>1027</fpage><lpage>1033</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Waszak, F., Hommel, B., &#x00026; Allport, A. (2004). Semantic generalization of stimulus-task bindings. Psychonomic Bulletin &#x00026; Review,11, 1027&#x02013;1033. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15875971</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waszak</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Hommel</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Allport</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Interaction of task readiness and automatic retrieval in task-switching: Negative priming and competitor priming</article-title><source>Memory &#x00026; Cognition</source><year>2005</year><volume>33</volume><fpage>595</fpage><lpage>610</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Waszak, F., Hommel, B., &#x00026; Allport, A. (2005). Interaction of task readiness and automatic retrieval in task-switching: Negative priming and competitor priming. Memory &#x00026; Cognition,33, 595&#x02013;610. </citation></ref><ref id="CR32"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>JHG</given-names></name><name><surname>Whiten</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Suddendorf</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Perrett</surname><given-names>DI</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Imitation, mirror neurons and autism</article-title><source>Neuroscience and Biobehavioral Reviews</source><year>2001</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>287</fpage><lpage>295</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0149-7634(01)00014-8</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Williams, J. H. G., Whiten, A., Suddendorf, T., &#x00026; Perrett, D. I. (2001). Imitation, mirror neurons and autism. Neuroscience and Biobehavioral Reviews,25, 287&#x02013;295. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11445135</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR33"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wylie</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Allport</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Task switching and the measurement of &#x0201c;switch costs&#x0201d;</article-title><source>Psychological Research</source><year>2000</year><volume>63</volume><fpage>212</fpage><lpage>233</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s004269900003</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wylie, G., &#x00026; Allport, A. (2000). Task switching and the measurement of &#x0201c;switch costs&#x0201d;. Psychological Research,63, 212&#x02013;233. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11004877</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="Fn1"><label>1</label><p>The two action tasks we used in test trials (i.e., precision and full grip) differed also in their movement direction. Recently, Brass et al. (2001b) reported that S&#x02013;R spatial compatibility related to movement direction also contributes to imitation facilitation. It is thus possible that this spatial component of our tasks (i.e., grasping the upper or the lower part of the manipulandum, respectively) influenced the congruency effects observed in the present study. Van Schie et al. (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR28">2008</xref>), however, demonstrated that the spatial compatibility of the task we used in our study affected general task execution in such a way that it facilitated tasks within the imitation context relative to the complementary context. They showed that the (reversal of the) congruency effect was independent of the spatial component present in this task.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>